# Day 5 — Pipelined RISC-V Core

## Overview

Day 5 focused on converting the RISC-V processor developed during the previous session into a pipelined processor.

The main objective was to improve instruction throughput by allowing different instructions to be processed at different stages of the processor at the same time.

## Pipelining

In a non-pipelined processor, an instruction completes its processing before the next instruction progresses through the processor.

Pipelining divides the processor operation into stages so that multiple instructions can occupy different stages simultaneously.

```text
Instruction 1 :  Fetch → Decode → Execute → Write
Instruction 2 :           Fetch → Decode → Execute → Write
Instruction 3 :                    Fetch → Decode → Execute → Write
```

This overlapping of operations improves the overall instruction throughput.

## Pipelining the RISC-V Core

The processor developed during Day 4 was reorganized into multiple pipeline stages.

Validity information was also used to distinguish valid and invalid instructions as they moved through the pipeline.

## Handling Hazards

Pipelining introduces situations where one instruction can depend on another instruction that is still being processed.

These situations are known as **pipeline hazards**.

### RAW Hazard

A Read-After-Write (RAW) hazard occurs when an instruction needs a value that is being produced by a previous instruction.

A bypassing technique can be used to provide the required value without unnecessarily waiting for the earlier instruction to complete.

### Branch Hazard

Branch instructions can change the normal flow of program execution.

The pipeline therefore needs to handle instructions that may have entered the pipeline before the branch decision was known.

Squashing or invalidating inappropriate instructions helps maintain correct execution.

## Load and Store Instructions

The processor was extended to support memory operations.

Load instructions transfer data from memory into a register, while store instructions transfer register data into memory.

A data-memory component is required to support these operations.

```text
Load:

Memory
   ↓
Register


Store:

Register
   ↓
Memory
```

## Completing the RISC-V Core

Additional instruction functionality was incorporated to complete the processor.

The final processor included the required logic for the base RISC-V integer instruction set along with memory operations and control-flow instructions.

## Verification

The completed design was tested using simulation and the workshop test programs.

The Makerchip environment and visualizations provided a way to observe processor behaviour and verify the implementation.

## Learning Outcomes

Through Day 5, I learned:

* The basic concept of processor pipelining.
* How instructions can overlap during execution.
* Why pipeline hazards occur.
* The purpose of bypassing for data hazards.
* How branch hazards can affect a pipeline.
* How load and store instructions interact with memory.
* How the different components come together to form a pipelined RISC-V processor.
* How simulation can be used to verify processor behaviour.

## Final Takeaway

Day 5 brought together the concepts developed throughout the workshop.

Starting from RISC-V instructions, the workshop progressed through software and assembly concepts, Makerchip and TL-Verilog, processor microarchitecture, and finally a pipelined RISC-V core.

```text
RISC-V ISA
    ↓
Assembly / ABI
    ↓
Makerchip + TL-Verilog
    ↓
RISC-V Microarchitecture
    ↓
Pipelined RISC-V Core
```

